650: cap \(C\), \(b=0\).  Its restriction on the intermediate product
651: compression is zero as well: \(H^2(W\times S^1)\) injects into the
652: cohomology of its two boundary components.  This is seen from
653: \(H_1(W)=\mathbf Z\), \(H_2(W)=\mathbf Z\), and the boundary
654: torus and sphere, whose products with the circle generate both
655: classes of \(H_2(W\times S^1)\).  On the two boundaries the relevant
656: restriction is zero.  Equivalently,
657: \(H^2(H_a)\to H^2(T^3)\) is injective, so the vanishing boundary
658: restriction of \(b\) gives its vanishing on this whole cap.
659: 
660: Extend \(M\) trivially across \(H_a\), with class \(m\).  The
661: Mayer–Vietoris difference \(b-2m\) belongs to
662: \(\delta H^1(T^3;\mathbf Z)\).  As in P3K.5, its square and its
663: product with \(m\) vanish.  Therefore
664: 
665: \[
666:  \langle b^2,[\bar X]\rangle=4m_{V,\mathrm{rel}}^2=-8,
667:  \qquad
668:  \sigma(\bar X)=\sigma(V)=-8,
669:  \qquad
670:  \langle p_1(T\bar X),[\bar X]\rangle=-24.
671: \]
672: 
673: These use the actual page invariants P3F.10.  No identification of
674: the two different marked caps is needed.
675: 
676: Finally \(T_{\mathrm{vert}}B\oplus\mathbf R\cong\pi^*\bar\nu\),
677: so \(e_v^2=\pi^*p_1(\bar\nu)\).  With \(\pi_*x=1\), this gives
678: 
679: \[
680:  \pi_*x^2=b,\qquad
681:  \pi_*x^3=\frac{p_1(\bar\nu)+3b^2}{4},\qquad
682:  p_1(TB)=\pi^*(p_1(T\bar X)+p_1(\bar\nu)).
683: \]
684: 
685: The actual spin return index is consequently
686: 
687: \[
688:  A(f)=\frac{4\int_Bx^3-\int_Bp_1(TB)x}{24}
689:      =\frac{3\int_{\bar X}b^2-\int_{\bar X}p_1(T\bar X)}{24}
690:      =\frac{3(-8)-(-24)}{24}=0.
691: \]
692: 
693: The normal Pontryagin class cancels.  Thus no unproved normal
694: framing or spin claim about the elliptic cap enters the result.
695: For the original parameter return, P3F.11 states that this covered
696: return has index six times the original integer.  Since that
697: integer is integral, the original index is also zero.  The actual
698: Hopf-lift gluing P3F.8 and the extending \(a\)-powers P3F.4 then
699: identify the clutched sphere as standard.  **End of proof.**
700: 
701: ## P3K.8. Proved: a second, analytic computation of the return index
702: 
703: The actual bordism of P3K.6–7 also gives an analytic computation of
704: \(A\) that does not evaluate the signature of the page, the square
705: of its line, or a Pontryagin number.  Keep the inner sphere bundle
706: \(\pi:B=S(\bar\nu)\to\bar X\) and the integral class
707: \(e_v=2x-\pi^*b\).
708: 
709: **Proof: vertical quantization.** The spin Dirac symbol on \(B\),
710: twisted by the Euler line of class \(x\), is a spin-c symbol of
711: determinant \(2x\).  The oriented vertical two-plane has its
712: canonical complex spin-c symbol, of determinant \(e_v\).  Dividing
713: these two spin-c structures gives the pullback of a base spin-c
714: structure of determinant \(b\).  This follows directly from the
715: stable tangent splitting into vertical and horizontal bundles and
716: the displayed determinant identity.  Fibrewise the vertical symbol
717: is the Dolbeault symbol of \(\mathcal O_{\mathbf P^1}\), not that of
718: \(\mathcal O(-1)\).  Its kernel consists of constant functions and
719: its cokernel is zero.  The constants are fixed by every oriented
720: sphere transition, so its family index is the trivial line, with
721: its actual transition functions.  This symbol composition can be
722: verified directly at the operator level.  Give the fibres their
723: round metrics and use an \(SO(3)\) connection on \(\bar\nu\).
724: The vertical Dolbeault Dirac operator \(D_v\) is invariant under
725: this structure group, so the horizontal connection commutes with
726: \(D_v\).  Its squared restriction orthogonal to the constants
727: has a positive uniform lower bound.  If \(\epsilon_v\) is the
728: vertical grading and \(D_h\) the horizontal base spin-c Dirac
729: operator on the bundle of vertical spinors, the operator
730: 
731: \[
732:  D_t=tD_v+\epsilon_vD_h,\qquad t>0,
733:  \qquad D_t^2=t^2D_v^2+D_h^2
734: \]
735: 
736: is invertible on that orthogonal complement.  The square identity
737: uses \(D_v\epsilon_v=-\epsilon_vD_v\) and the commuting invariant
738: connection.  On the constant vertical kernel it is precisely the
739: base Dirac operator.  The displayed operator has the same elliptic
740: symbol as the twisted total-space spin Dirac operator after a
741: positive rescaling of the vertical metric; zeroth-order terms do
742: not change its index.  Consequently
743: 
744: \[
745:  A=\operatorname{ind}(D_{\bar X,b}).
746: \]
747: 
748: This is a factorization of the specified actual symbol.  It keeps
749: the meridian Dirac index one that is essential at the conifold
750: replacement.
751: 
752: **Proof: switch the marked cap.** The compression cap is
753: \(H_a=D^2_a\times S^1_b\times S^1_\gamma\), whereas the regular
754: elliptic cap is \(H_\gamma=S^1_a\times S^1_b\times D^2_\gamma\).
755: Use their common marked \(T^3\) boundary.  Their closed difference
756: is
757: 
758: \[
759:  H_a\cup_{T^3}(-H_\gamma)
760:  =S^1_b\times
761:    \bigl((D^2_a\times S^1_\gamma)
762:        \cup_{T^2}(S^1_a\times D^2_\gamma)\bigr)
763:  \cong S^1\times S^3.
764: \]
765: 
766: The indicated meridian-longitude exchange is the usual two-solid-
767: torus decomposition of \(S^3\); its meridian intersection is one.
768: Both cap determinant classes vanish and their boundary spin-c
769: structures agree: on \(T^3\), a spin-c structure with determinant
770: class zero is unique because \(H^2(T^3;\mathbf Z)\) has no
771: two-torsion.  Glue with this boundary identification.  The closed
772: difference has \(H^2=0\), so it has its spin structure as a spin-c
773: structure and extends over \(S^1\times D^4\).  Its Dirac index is
774: zero by spin-c bordism.
775: 
776: For completeness, additivity here uses the cut-and-paste bordism
777: obtained by taking the cylinders on the common page and on the
778: two caps, and joining their common boundary collars by a pair of
779: pants.  The three boundary components are the old capped page,
780: the oppositely oriented new capped page, and the oppositely
781: oriented closed cap difference.  The same spin-c structure on the
782: common collar extends over this product construction.  Thus the
783: index of \((\bar X,b)\) is equal to that of the compact sectioned
784: elliptic page \(S\) with the resulting determinant \(b_\gamma\).
785: 
786: **Proof: identify the base spin-c structure.** The two integral
787: vanishing cycles needed here can be exhibited directly.  In the
788: source marking of P3F.12, take
789: 
790: \[
791:  A_E=\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\-1&-1\end{pmatrix},\qquad
792:  B_E=\begin{pmatrix}0&-1\\1&0\end{pmatrix}.
793: \]
794: 
795: The nodal monodromy \(A_EB_E\) has image of its nilpotent part
796: generated by \(a=(0,1)^t\).  The twelve unramified cusp lifts of
797: the regular-binding page include the transport \(A_Ea=(1,-1)^t\).
798: The two columns \(a,A_Ea\) have determinant minus one.  Van Kampen
799: therefore kills the whole fundamental group of a torus fibre.
800: The section then identifies the fundamental group of the compact
801: elliptic page with that of its genus-three base, and identifies
802: the fundamental group of the open page with that of the base
803: with one boundary component.  In particular \(H_1(S;\mathbf Z)
804: \cong\mathbf Z^6\), so \(H^2(S;\mathbf Z)\) has no torsion.
805: 
806: On the marked boundary \(T^3\), the two source loops die in
807: \(H_1(V)\), and the base boundary loop is a product of
808: commutators and also dies.  Hence the restriction
809: \(H^1(V)\to H^1(T^3)\) is zero.  The image of
810: \(H^1(H_\gamma)\) is the span of the two source characters.
811: The Mayer–Vietoris kernel of restriction to \(V\) and
812: \(H_\gamma\) is consequently the infinite cyclic group generated
813: by the fibre class \(F\).  It is primitive, since the section
814: intersects a fibre once.
815: 
816: The elliptic canonical class \(K_S\) is pulled back from the base,
817: so it restricts to zero on both the page with boundary and the
818: regular cap.  The actual determinant and the canonical spin-c
819: determinant twisted by the Picard line thus satisfy
820: 
821: \[
822:  b_\gamma-\bigl(-K_S+2c_1(M)\bigr)=kF
823: \]
824: 
825: for an integer \(k\).  Both are characteristic classes.  Since
826: \(F\) is primitive, their common mod-two class makes \(k=2n\)
827: even.  Therefore
828: 
829: \[
830:  b_\gamma=-K_S+2c_1(M+nF).
831: \]
832: 
833: There is no two-torsion ambiguity in the spin-c structure on
834: \(S\).  Its Dirac operator is exactly the Dolbeault spin-c
835: operator twisted by \(M+nF\).
836: 
837: **Proof: acyclicity from explicit sections.** The source
838: primitive-generator identification fixes, up to sign,
839: 
840: \[
841:  P_*=(0,\sqrt2\,t^2(t-1))
842:  \quad\hbox{on}\quad
843:  y^2=x^3-3t^3(t-1)x+2t^4(t-1)^2.
844: \]
845: 
846: We use that identification only to specify the line in the
847: construction.  The following direct calculation of its fibrewise
848: nontriviality does not use the height, the signature, or the square
849: of the page line.  In the ordinary affine charts the displayed
850: point is finite and hence different from the zero section.  At the
851: \(IV^*\) fibre take \(t=z^3\), \(x=z^4X\), \(y=z^6Y\).
852: Then
853: 
854: \[
855:  P'_*=(0,\sqrt2(z^3-1))\longrightarrow(0,-\sqrt2)
856:  \quad\hbox{on }Y^2=X^3+2.
857: \]
858: 
859: At the \(III\) fibre take \(t=1+z^4\), \(x=z^2X\),
860: \(y=z^3Y\).  This gives
861: 
862: \[
863:  P'_*=(0,\sqrt2\,z(1+z^4)^2)\longrightarrow(0,0)
864:  \quad\hbox{on }Y^2=X^3-3X.
865: \]
866: 
867: Both limiting points are finite smooth points of the good-reduction
868: elliptic curves and are different from the point at infinity.
869: At the nodal fibre put \(q=1/t\), \(X=q^2x\), \(Y=q^3y\).
870: The section is \((0,\sqrt2(1-q))\), with limiting point
871: \((0,\sqrt2)\) on \(Y^2=(X-1)^2(X+2)\).  It is smooth and
872: different from both the node \((1,0)\) and the zero section.
873: The same statements hold for \(-P_*\); fibre inversion gives
874: the exact comparison between these two choices.  Pullback under
875: the actual regular-binding cover preserves these explicit
876: sections and their nonidentity specializations.  Every smooth
877: elliptic fibre therefore carries a nontrivial degree-zero line,
878: whose zeroth and first cohomology vanish.  On each of the twelve
879: nodal fibres the line has degree zero and a nonidentity
880: multiplicative Picard parameter.  The normalization sequence is
881: a two-term complex
882: \(\mathbf C\xrightarrow{1-\lambda}\mathbf C\), where
883: \(\lambda\ne1\); it is acyclic.  The bundle \(nF\) is pulled
884: back from the base and does not affect any of these restrictions.
885: All source identities and root-chart substitutions are checked
886: exactly in `code/part3_fourfold/direct_section.py` and its recorded
887: outputs.
888: Proper cohomology and base change give the vanishing below.
889: More precisely, Stacks Project Lemma 30.22.1, tag 07VK,
890: applies to this proper flat projective elliptic fibration and
891: its line bundle: on each Noetherian affine base open it provides
892: a perfect complex computing the direct image and commuting with
893: arbitrary derived base change.  Every fibre complex is zero by
894: the preceding calculation.  A bounded complex of finite projective
895: modules with this property is zero: at any local ring, cancel
896: every differential entry that is a unit; the remaining minimal
897: complex has zero differentials after reduction to the residue
898: field, so its zero fibre cohomology forces every remaining rank
899: to be zero.  This proves
900: 
901: \[
902:  R f_*(M+nF)=0.
903: \]
904: 
905: Consequently the Dolbeault index is zero.  Combining the actual
906: symbol factorization and cap comparison gives
907: 
908: \[
909:  A=\operatorname{ind}(D_{\bar X,b})
910:   =\operatorname{ind}\bar\partial_{S,M+nF}=0.
911: \]
912: 
913: The computation uses fibrewise acyclicity and the explicit cap
914: switch; it does not reuse the numerical page characteristic
915: calculation in P3K.7.  The integral cover argument P3F.11 again
916: gives zero for the original parameter return.  **End of proof.**
917: 
918: ## P3K.9. Proved: the rotating normal framing and the individual return integers
919: 
920: For the rotating framing specified below, the one-gamma-turn
921: return has Jin characteristic numbers \((k,\ell)=(-6,-24)\).
922: After removing an extending constant-rotation loop, this framing
923: descends through the sixfold parameter cover.  With the descended
924: framing the original positive return has
925: 
926: \[
927:  k=-1,\qquad \ell=-4.
928: \]
929: 
930: Every other tubular framing differs by the explicitly described
